============================================================================================================
HOW TO READ THIS
============================================================================================================
  vs the no-model reversion rule:
    net/trade   model $  -3.88   revert $  -3.04
    gross/trade model $   0.61   revert $   0.28
    cost/trade  model $   4.49   revert $   3.32

  The model loses on NET but wins on GROSS ($0.61 vs $0.28, 2.2x).
  So this is NOT 'the signal is no better than mean reversion'. The
  signal is better; the trades cost $+1.17 more. The entry gate
  fires on predicted dislocation, dislocation grows with leg count, and
  so do fees and spread crossings -- signal strength and execution cost
  share a common cause, so selecting on one selects for the other.
  Report it as a trade-SELECTION failure, not a forecasting failure.

  If NET is negative everywhere while gross is positive, the forecast edge is
  real and smaller than the cost of acting on it. That is a legitimate and
  publishable finding -- it is the same shape as the ladder result, and it is
  the honest answer to 'is this strategy profitable'.

  Compare model against static_arb, not against zero. static_arb crosses each
  leg ONCE because settlement is free; the round trip crosses twice. A forecast
  strategy that loses to holding the static arbitrage has not earned its
  complexity even if its own net is positive.
